{"parentUuid":"169c3c60-64e4-4f18-9b39-be33999ce4ee","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"fbd7c847-bb13-4c9d-8b3d-9d904e035a94","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LrCSMe7Dt4pEb8hiQx75BJ","type":"tool_result","content":"=== cell 0 [markdown] ===\n# Qoro × PushQuantum Hackathon — 02 · Network design (QOBLIB 08)\n\n[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/QoroQuantum/pushquantum-hackathon-2026/blob/main/02_network_design.ipynb)\n\nBuild a directed network in which every node has exactly two outgoing and two incoming links, so that\nwhen all traffic is routed, the busiest link carries as little as possible. A QUBO proposes network\ntopologies, and an exact linear program scores them. This notebook builds that pipeline, compares\nquantum and classical proposers, and writes a solution file in QOBLIB's format.\n\n**Your challenge: scale up.** Instances run from `network05` (20 binary variables) to `network24`\n(552). Up to `network10` the optimum is proven; from `network11` on, only best-known values exist\n(section 1.5). Solve the largest instance you can, as close to the best-known value as you can.\n\n**Scoring** uses QOBLIB's official objective, not your QUBO's energy: `qt.route_min_congestion`\nreproduces every published QOBLIB value. Treat it as the referee.\n\n**Before you start,** work through notebook 00 (setup and QUBO 101).\n=== cell 1 [markdown] ===\n---\n## 0 · Setup\n\nRun these cells first. Notebook 00 (Part 0) explains them: your API key and the install. The helper file\n`qoblib_tools.py` is downloaded for you.\n=== cell 2 [code] ===\n# Setup, explained in notebook 00 (Part 0). If this cell installs Divi, restart the session\n# (Runtime → Restart session) and run the notebook from the top.\nimport os\nfrom importlib.metadata import PackageNotFoundError, version\n\ntry:\n    divi_version = version(\"qoro-divi\")\nexcept PackageNotFoundError:\n    divi_version = None\nif divi_version != \"0.16.2\":\n    %pip install -q \"qoro-divi[qubo-decompose,jupyter]==0.16.2\"\n    print(\"Installed Divi 0.16.2: restart the session and run from the top.\")\n\ntry:  # Google Colab: copy the secret QORO_API_KEY into the environment\n    from google.colab import userdata\n    os.environ[\"QORO_API_KEY\"] = userdata.get(\"QORO_API_KEY\")\nexcept ImportError:\n    pass  # not in Colab: Divi reads .env or the environment by itself\nexcept Exception as err:  # no secret yet, or notebook access switched off\n    print(f\"No QORO_API_KEY in Colab Secrets yet ({type(err).__name__}): see notebook 00, section 0.1.\")\n=== cell 3 [code] ===\n# The helper file qoblib_tools.py (instance loaders and official scorers) is downloaded from the\n# hackathon repository if it is not next to this notebook yet.\nimport urllib.request\n\nif not os.path.exists(\"qoblib_tools.py\"):\n    urllib.request.urlretrieve(\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/QoroQuantum/pushquantum-hackathon-2026/main/qoblib_tools.py\", \"qoblib_tools.py\")\nimport qoblib_tools as qt\n\nmissing = [name for name in [\n    \"load_network_demand\", \"route_min_congestion\", \"build_network_qubo\", \"repair_topology\",\n    \"route_integral\", \"write_network_solution\", \"check_network_solution\",\n] if not hasattr(qt, name)]\nif missing:\n    raise ImportError(\n        f\"{os.path.relpath(qt.__file__)} is an old copy (no {', '.join(missing)}). Delete it, restart the \"\n        \"session and run from the top: the current version is downloaded.\"\n    )\nprint(\"qoblib_tools loaded from\", os.path.relpath(qt.__file__))\n--- out: qoblib_tools loaded from qoblib_tools.py\n\n=== cell 4 [code] ===\nimport itertools\nimport time\nimport warnings\n\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\", category=SyntaxWarning)  # noisy docstrings in some dependencies\n\nimport hybrid\nimport numpy as np\nimport requests\nfrom dwave.samplers import SimulatedAnnealingSampler\nfrom IPython.display import display\n\nfrom divi.backends import JobConfig, MaestroSimulator, QoroService\nfrom divi.backends.characterization import CharacterizationOptions, characterize_and_validate\nfrom divi.qprog.algorithms import PCE, QAOA, PolyEncoding, SoftObjective\nfrom divi.qprog.ensembles import BeamSearchStrategy, PartitioningProgramEnsemble\nfrom divi.qprog.optimizers import MonteCarloOptimizer\nfrom divi.qprog.problems import (\n    BinaryOptimizationProblem,\n    CommunityDecomposer,\n    LinearConstraint,\n    QUBOPartitioningConfig,\n)\n\nprint(\"qoro-divi\", version(\"qoro-divi\"))\n--- out: qoro-divi 0.16.2\n\n=== cell 5 [code] ===\nUSE_CLOUD = False            # False: local Maestro simulator · True: Qoro cloud (uses credits)\nRUN_CHARACTERIZATION = True  # Characterization Service calls (cloud; cost scales with QUBO size)\nSHOTS = 10_000\nMC_POPULATION = 50          # Monte Carlo optimiser: circuits per iteration, sent to the backend as one batch\nMC_ITERATIONS = 10          # Monte Carlo optimiser iterations\n\ntry:\n    service = QoroService()  # reads QORO_API_KEY from .env or the environment\n    balance = service.get_credit_balance()\n    print(\"Connected to Qoro. Credit balance:\", balance.get(\"balance\", balance))\nexcept (ValueError, requests.RequestException) as err:  # no key, a wrong key, or no connection\n    service = None\n    print(f\"{err}\\nNo working API key (notebook 00, section 0.1): cloud cells will be skipped.\")\nif USE_CLOUD and service is None:\n    raise RuntimeError(\"USE_CLOUD = True needs a working API key: see notebook 00, section 0.1.\")\n\n\ndef make_backend(shots: int = SHOTS, force_sampling: bool = False):\n    # force_sampling=True: estimate everything from shots instead of exact expectation values\n    if USE_CLOUD:\n        return QoroService(\n            job_config=JobConfig(\n                shots=shots, simulator_cluster=\"qoro_maestro\", force_sampling=force_sampling\n            )\n        )\n    return MaestroSimulator(shots=shots, force_sampling=force_sampling)\n\n\nbackend = make_backend()\nprint(\"Circuits run on:\", type(backend).__name__)\n--- out: Connected to Qoro. Credit balance: 3979.89\nCircuits run on: MaestroSimulator\n\n=== cell 6 [markdown] ===\n**`solve_partitioned`** is the helper from notebook 00 (section 1.5), with one addition: an optional\n`decomposer` argument, for when you want to choose the partitions yourself.\n=== cell 7 [code] ===\ndef solve_partitioned(bqm, routine=\"qaoa\", cluster_size=12, n_layers=2, backend=backend, decomposer=None, **routine_kwargs):\n    # Partition `bqm`, solve every part with `routine`, and return the ensemble.\n    # routine: \"qaoa\" | \"iterative_qaoa\" | \"pce\" | \"progressive_pce\"\n    problem = BinaryOptimizationProblem(\n        bqm,\n        decomposer=decomposer or CommunityDecomposer(\n            QUBOPartitioningConfig(max_n_variables_per_cluster=cluster_size)\n        ),\n        composer=hybrid.SplatComposer(),\n        local_search=True,\n    )\n    ensemble = PartitioningProgramEnsemble(\n        problem=problem,\n        quantum_routine=routine,\n        n_layers=n_layers,\n        optimizer=MonteCarloOptimizer(population_size=MC_POPULATION),\n        max_iterations=MC_ITERATIONS,\n        backend=backend,\n        **routine_kwargs,\n    )\n    ensemble.run()\n    return ensemble\n=== cell 8 [markdown] ===\n---\n## 1 · QOBLIB 08: network design\n\nGiven an $n \\times n$ traffic demand matrix $t_{ij}$, build a directed network on $n$ nodes in which\n**every node has exactly 2 outgoing and 2 incoming links** (no self-loops). Route all traffic, letting\nflows split over several paths, and **minimise the largest total flow on any link**. The score is that\nmaximum × 1000, as an integer. Instances `network05` … `network24` use the top-left $n \\times n$\nblock of one fixed demand matrix.\n\nThe problem splits into two parts (credit to the parallel-tempering submission on QOBLIB):\n\n* **Choosing the topology** is combinatorial: one binary variable per possible link, $n(n-1)$ in\n  total. This is the QUBO part.\n* **Routing on a fixed topology** is a linear program that `qt.route_min_congestion` solves exactly in\n  milliseconds. It reproduces every published QOBLIB value.\n\nSo the quantum or QUBO solver *proposes* topologies, and the LP *scores* them.\n=== cell 9 [code] ===\nN_NODES = 5\ndemand = qt.load_network_demand(N_NODES)\nprint(\"Demand matrix (traffic from row node to column node):\\n\", demand)\n\nref_path = qt.fetch_qoblib_file(f\"08-network/solutions/network{N_NODES:02d}.opt.sol\")\n_, ref_arcs, _ = qt.read_network_solution(ref_path)\nprint(\"\\nPublished optimal topology:\", [(i + 1, j + 1) for i, j in ref_arcs])\nprint(\"Its score:\", qt.route_min_congestion(demand, ref_arcs).objective)\nprint(\"A random valid topology:\", qt.route_min_congestion(demand, qt.random_topology(N_NODES, np.random.default_rng(0))).objective)\n--- out: Demand matrix (traffic from row node to column node):\n [[ 0 24 43 23 21]\n [ 0  0  0  0 21]\n [16  0  0 20 44]\n [42 20 18  0 40]\n [ 0  0  0 40  0]]\n\nPublished optimal topology: [(1, 3), (1, 2), (2, 5), (2, 3), (3, 5), (3, 4), (4, 1), (4, 2), (5, 1), (5, 4)]\nIts score: 65500.0\nA random valid topology: 83000.0\n\n=== cell 10 [markdown] ===\nFor `network05` there are few enough topologies to score every one, giving a ground truth for the\npipeline below.\n=== cell 11 [code] ===\nrows = [list(itertools.combinations([j for j in range(N_NODES) if j != i], 2)) for i in range(N_NODES)]\nall_topologies = []\nfor choice in itertools.product(*rows):\n    arcs = [(i, j) for i, pair in enumerate(choice) for j in pair]\n    if qt.is_valid_topology(N_NODES, arcs):\n        all_topologies.append(arcs)\nall_scores = np.array([qt.route_min_congestion(demand, a).objective for a in all_topologies])\nprint(f\"{len(all_topologies)} valid topologies; best {all_scores.min():.0f}, median {np.median(all_scores):.0f}, worst {all_scores.max():.0f}\")\n--- out: 216 valid topologies; best 65500, median 83000, worst 115500\n\n=== cell 12 [markdown] ===\n### 1.1 A topology QUBO with Divi's `LinearConstraint`\n\n`qt.build_network_qubo` gives a **surrogate** objective. It rewards links that carry their own demand\ndirectly: $-\\sum t_{ij} x_{ij}$, scaled. The real objective needs the LP and is not a QUBO, so\n**designing a better surrogate is a big part of the challenge.**\n\nThe degree rules are linear equalities. Instead of hand-building penalties, hand them to Divi as\n`LinearConstraint`s: `BinaryOptimizationProblem` adds them as squared penalties, and\n`problem.is_feasible(bitstring)` checks them. QAOA's `get_top_solutions(feasibility=\"filter\")` keeps\nonly the feasible samples, ranked by the true objective rather than the penalised one.\n=== cell 13 [code] ===\nsurrogate, degree_penalty, arcs = qt.build_network_qubo(demand)  # arcs[v] = link of variable v\nconstraints = []\nfor node in range(N_NODES):\n    constraints.append(LinearConstraint({v: 1 for v, (i, j) in enumerate(arcs) if i == node}, \"==\", 2))  # out\n    constraints.append(LinearConstraint({v: 1 for v, (i, j) in enumerate(arcs) if j == node}, \"==\", 2))  # in\nnet_problem = BinaryOptimizationProblem(surrogate, constraints=constraints, penalty_weight=2.0)\nprint(f\"{len(arcs)} binary variables → {net_problem.cost_hamiltonian.num_qubits} qubits\")\n--- out: 20 binary variables → 20 qubits\n\n=== cell 14 [markdown] ===\n### 1.2 Characterize & validate, then run QAOA\n\nBefore spending circuits, send `net_problem` to the **Characterization Service** (notebook 00, section\n1.2) with the **published optimal topology as the reference state**. That validates the surrogate: if\nthe true optimum sits high in the QUBO's energy landscape, no sampler will find it through this QUBO,\nand the surrogate needs work. On `network05` the published optimum is the QUBO's ground state.\n\nThe constraints count as the penalty component, so the report has a `penalty` section with a\nrecommended weight, and its `structure` section lists the ten degree rules as *penalty blocks*: groups\nof 4 variables that must hold exactly 2 links each. The call can take several minutes. Divi warns that\nthe QUBO's constant offset isn't sent: the reported energies exclude it, and nothing else changes.\n=== cell 15 [code] ===\nopt_arcs = set(map(tuple, ref_arcs))  # the published optimal topology, loaded above\nref_bits = \"\".join(\"1\" if tuple(arc) in opt_arcs else \"0\" for arc in arcs)\n\nnet_report = None\nif service is not None and RUN_CHARACTERIZATION:\n    net_report = characterize_and_validate(\n        net_problem,\n        reference_states=[ref_bits],\n        service=service,\n        options=CharacterizationOptions(ansatz={\"layers\": 2}),\n    )\n    display(net_report)\n    print(\"published optimum:  \", net_report.references[0])\n    print(\"penalty section:    \", net_report.penalty)\n    print(\"penalty blocks:     \", len(net_report.structure[\"penalty_blocks\"]))\n    print(\"recommended layers: \", net_report.recommended_layers)\nelse:\n    print(\"Skipped: no API key, or RUN_CHARACTERIZATION is False.\")\n--- out: /tmp/ipykernel_50644/235482186.py:6: UserWarning: The problem carries a constant offset, which is not transmitted. The analysis is unaffected — a constant shifts every energy equally — but the reported energies exclude it.\n  net_report = characterize_and_validate(\n\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: CharacterizationResult(job_id='0e4aa6e7-bd72-4c9a-a5c1-dc3c8a22a5a3', status='COMPLETED')\n  The exact optimum is already known classically; use QAOA here to tune or benchmark it before scaling up.\n  [action] Use a penalty weight λ ≈ 0.4958: the ground state is feasible there with the widest gap to the next energy level.\n  [info] The penalty is 10 blocks each holding a group of 4 variables to a fixed count set (listed under structure): an XY mixer over each group, started from a state with that count, keeps it satisfied without its penalty terms.\n  [info] At the requested p = 2 layers QAOA's mean energy closes 68.9% of the gap from random guessing to the optimum.\n  [info] This report's QAOA results are at the submitted QUBO's λ = 2; rebuild the QUBO with λ ≈ 0.4958 and resubmit to see QAOA there.\n--- out: published optimum:   {'energy': -85.77272727272727, 'bitstring': '11000101001111001001', 'gap_to_best': 0.0, 'probability': 0.0008193272041915764}\npenalty section:     {'exact': True, 'qaoa_lambda': 2.0, 'safe_lambda': 1.3409090909090913, 'search_range': [0.01, 100.0], 'penalty_to_cost': 56.774193548387096, 'recommended_lambda': 0.49575268420302737, 'min_feasible_lambda': 0.4545454665227432}\npenalty blocks:      10\nrecommended layers:  6\n\n=== cell 16 [markdown] ===\nQAOA runs twice, as in notebook 00: **blind** (Monte Carlo from random starting angles) and, if you ran the\ncharacterization, **warm-started** from the service's angles at its recommended layer count, plus small random offsets. Every\nsample is checked against the degree rules, and every valid topology is scored by the LP.\n=== cell 17 [code] ===\ndef run_network_qaoa(label, n_layers, initial_params=None):\n    t0 = time.time()\n    qaoa_net = QAOA(\n        net_problem,\n        n_layers=n_layers,\n        optimizer=MonteCarloOptimizer(population_size=MC_POPULATION),\n        max_iterations=MC_ITERATIONS,\n        backend=make_backend(shots=20_000),\n        seed=3,\n    )\n    qaoa_net.run(initial_params=initial_params)\n    valid = qaoa_net.get_top_solutions(n=0, feasibility=\"filter\")  # n=0: every feasible sample\n    scores = {s.bitstring: qt.route_min_congestion(demand, qt.decode_topology(s.bitstring, arcs)).objective for s in valid}\n    p_opt = sum(s.prob for s in valid if scores[s.bitstring] == all_scores.min())\n    best = min(scores.values())\n    print(f\"{label:<24} {qaoa_net.total_circuit_count:>3} circuits, {time.time() - t0:>3.0f}s | {len(valid)} valid topologies, \"\n          f\"{sum(s.prob for s in valid):5.1%} of the probability mass | P(optimal topology) {p_opt:.3%} | \"\n          f\"best sampled: {best:.0f}, rank {(all_scores < best).sum() + 1} of {len(all_topologies)}\")\n\n\nrun_network_qaoa(\"QAOA p=2, blind\", n_layers=2)\ninit = net_report.qaoa_initial_params() if net_report is not None else None\nif init is not None:\n    p = init.shape[1] // 2\n    # One starting point per population member: the service's angles plus small random offsets.\n    starts = init + np.random.default_rng(3).normal(scale=0.05, size=(MC_POPULATION, 2 * p))\n    starts[0] = init[0]\n    run_network_qaoa(f\"QAOA p={p}, warm-started\", n_layers=p, initial_params=starts)\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: QAOA p=2, blind          501 circuits,  58s | 180 valid topologies,  2.0% of the probability mass | P(optimal topology) 0.005% | best sampled: 65500, rank 1 of 216\n\n--- out: QAOA p=6, warm-started   501 circuits, 128s | 216 valid topologies, 75.5% of the probability mass | P(optimal topology) 0.580% | best sampled: 65500, rank 1 of 216\n\n=== cell 18 [markdown] ===\n### 1.3 Scale up: `network12`\n\n132 binary variables. We compare four ways of proposing topologies, each scored by the LP:\n\n* random valid topologies,\n* simulated annealing on the surrogate QUBO,\n* partitioned QAOA on the surrogate QUBO,\n* PCE on the *whole* surrogate QUBO: 132 variables on 16 qubits.\n\nSamples can break the degree rules. Partitions cut straight through them, and single-bit flips\ncan't fix a node with one link too many when the other end already has exactly two.\n`qt.repair_topology` maps any sample to the closest valid topology, keeping as many of its links as\npossible, so every sample becomes a scorable candidate.\n=== cell 19 [code] ===\nN_BIG, BKV_NET = 12, 276474\ndemand_big = qt.load_network_demand(N_BIG)\nsur_big, pen_big, arcs_big = qt.build_network_qubo(demand_big)\nnet_bqm = sur_big.copy()\npen = pen_big.copy()\npen.scale(1.0)  # penalty weight\nnet_bqm.update(pen)\n\n\ndef best_topology(samples, arcs, demand):\n    # Decode → repair → LP-score every sample; return (best score, best arcs, #distinct topologies).\n    n = len(demand)\n    topologies = {\n        tuple(sorted(qt.repair_topology(n, qt.decode_topology(s, arcs), demand))) for s in samples\n    }\n    scores = {t: qt.route_min_congestion(demand, t).objective for t in topologies}\n    best = min(scores, key=scores.get)\n    return scores[best], list(best), len(topologies)\n\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nrandom_best = min(qt.route_min_congestion(demand_big, qt.random_topology(N_BIG, rng)).objective for _ in range(300))\nprint(f\"Random (300 topologies):          {random_best:.0f}\")\n\nsa_net = SimulatedAnnealingSampler().sample(net_bqm, num_reads=300, seed=1)\nsa_net_score, sa_net_arcs, n_valid = best_topology(sa_net.samples(), arcs_big, demand_big)\nprint(f\"Simulated annealing on surrogate: {sa_net_score:.0f} ({n_valid} distinct topologies)\")\n\nt0 = time.time()\nnet_bqm_n = net_bqm.copy()\nnet_bqm_n.normalize()\nnet_ensemble = solve_partitioned(net_bqm_n, \"qaoa\", cluster_size=12)\nnet_tops = net_ensemble.get_top_solutions(\n    n=100, strategy=BeamSearchStrategy(beam_width=100, n_partition_candidates=10)\n)\nq_net_score, q_net_arcs, n_valid = best_topology(\n    [dict(zip(net_bqm_n.variables, solution)) for solution, _ in net_tops], arcs_big, demand_big\n)\nprint(f\"Partitioned QAOA on surrogate:    {q_net_score:.0f} ({n_valid} distinct topologies, {time.time() - t0:.0f}s)\")\n\nt0 = time.time()\npce_net = PCE(\n    BinaryOptimizationProblem(net_bqm_n),\n    encoding=PolyEncoding(),\n    objective=SoftObjective(alpha=2.0),\n    n_layers=2,\n    optimizer=MonteCarloOptimizer(population_size=MC_POPULATION),\n    max_iterations=MC_ITERATIONS,\n    backend=backend,\n    seed=1,\n)\npce_net.run()\npce_tops = pce_net.get_top_solutions(n=100, min_prob=0.0, sort_by=\"energy\")\npce_net_score, pce_net_arcs, n_valid = best_topology([s.bitstring for s in pce_tops], arcs_big, demand_big)\nprint(f\"PCE on surrogate ({pce_net.n_qubits} qubits):     {pce_net_score:.0f} ({n_valid} distinct topologies, {time.time() - t0:.0f}s)\")\nprint(f\"Best known:                       {BKV_NET}\")\n--- out: Random (300 topologies):          328400\nSimulated annealing on surrogate: 309750 (300 distinct topologies)\n\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: Partitioned QAOA on surrogate:    347000 (68 distinct topologies, 40s)\n\n--- out: PCE on surrogate (16 qubits):     313778 (100 distinct topologies, 8s)\nBest known:                       276474\n\n=== cell 20 [markdown] ===\n### 1.4 Write a submission file\n\nThe checker wants **integer** flows, in units of 1/1000. `qt.route_integral` finds integer flows that\nachieve the LP score, and `qt.check_network_solution` is a port of QOBLIB's checker.\n=== cell 21 [code] ===\nbest_net_score, best_net_arcs = min(\n    (sa_net_score, sa_net_arcs), (q_net_score, q_net_arcs), (pce_net_score, pce_net_arcs), key=lambda x: x[0]\n)\nrouting = qt.route_integral(demand_big, best_net_arcs)\nnet_path = f\"network{N_BIG:02d}.sol\"\nz = qt.write_network_solution(net_path, N_BIG, routing)\nprint(f\"Wrote {net_path} with objective {z}\")\nprint(\"Checker:\", qt.check_network_solution(net_path, N_BIG, demand_big))\n--- out: Wrote network12.sol with objective 309750\nChecker: (True, 309750, 'valid')\n\n=== cell 22 [markdown] ===\n### 1.5 Targets and ideas\n\nBest-known values from the [QOBLIB table](https://github.com/ZIB-AOPT/QOBLIB/blob/main/08-network/solutions/README.md) (check it for updates).\n\n| Instance | Variables | Best known | Status |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| `network05` … `network10` | 20 – 90 | 65 500 … 210 800 | proven optimal: match them |\n| `network11`, `network13` … `network23` | 110 – 506 | 238 334 … 659 369 | best known, set by a parallel-tempering submission |\n| `network12` | 132 | 276 474 | best known (reference) |\n| `network24` | 552 | 663 688 | best known (reference) |\n\n**Ideas to try**\n* **Better surrogates.** Reward two-hop paths $x_{ij}x_{jk}$ for large $t_{ik}$. Penalise nodes that\n  send a lot of traffic over few links. Learn the weights from LP results.\n* **LP feedback (Benders-style).** The LP's dual values tell you which links are congested. Re-weight\n  the QUBO and re-solve in a loop.\n* **Feasible-by-construction encodings.** A valid topology is two permutations with no fixed points\n  that never agree. One-hot permutation encodings with an XY mixer keep QAOA inside the feasible space. The report in section 1.2 suggests the same: an XY mixer over each\n  penalty block, started from a state with 2 links.\n* **Beat SA first.** On `network12` the plain quantum pipelines above are far from the best known\n  value, and simulated annealing on the same surrogate is a strong bar. Closing that gap with better surrogates, penalties, depth or encodings is a good first goal.\n* **Quantum proposals inside a local search.** Use QAOA or PCE samples to propose degree-preserving\n  2-exchanges, $(a\\to b), (c\\to d) \\Rightarrow (a\\to d), (c\\to b)$, scored by the LP.\n* **Characterize at scale.** Section 1.2 characterizes `network05`. Do the same for the instance you\n  are pushing: read `structure` to choose partitions and `penalty` for the weight, and pass your best\n  topology as a reference state to check that your surrogate ranks it well.\n=== cell 23 [markdown] ===\n---\n## Handing in\n\nKeep every `.sol` file you write, with the instance, its size and the method and settings that produced\nit. **Handing in** at the end of notebook 00 says how to submit.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"96978f36-2a91-4e2b-8f23-d94042f4b0ce","timestamp":"2026-11-21T20:09:46.118Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== cell 0 [markdown] ===\n# Qoro × PushQuantum Hackathon — 02 · Network design (QOBLIB 08)\n\n[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/QoroQuantum/pushquantum-hackathon-2026/blob/main/02_network_design.ipynb)\n\nBuild a directed network in which every node has exactly two outgoing and two incoming links, so that\nwhen all traffic is routed, the busiest link carries as little as possible. A QUBO proposes network\ntopologies, and an exact linear program scores them. This notebook builds that pipeline, compares\nquantum and classical proposers, and writes a solution file in QOBLIB's format.\n\n**Your challenge: scale up.** Instances run from `network05` (20 binary variables) to `network24`\n(552). Up to `network10` the optimum is proven; from `network11` on, only best-known values exist\n(section 1.5). Solve the largest instance you can, as close to the best-known value as you can.\n\n**Scoring** uses QOBLIB's official objective, not your QUBO's energy: `qt.route_min_congestion`\nreproduces every published QOBLIB value. Treat it as the referee.\n\n**Before you start,** work through notebook 00 (setup and QUBO 101).\n=== cell 1 [markdown] ===\n---\n## 0 · Setup\n\nRun these cells first. Notebook 00 (Part 0) explains them: your API key and the install. The helper file\n`qoblib_tools.py` is downloaded for you.\n=== cell 2 [code] ===\n# Setup, explained in notebook 00 (Part 0). If this cell installs Divi, restart the session\n# (Runtime → Restart session) and run the notebook from the top.\nimport os\nfrom importlib.metadata import PackageNotFoundError, version\n\ntry:\n    divi_version = version(\"qoro-divi\")\nexcept PackageNotFoundError:\n    divi_version = None\nif divi_version != \"0.16.2\":\n    %pip install -q \"qoro-divi[qubo-decompose,jupyter]==0.16.2\"\n    print(\"Installed Divi 0.16.2: restart the session and run from the top.\")\n\ntry:  # Google Colab: copy the secret QORO_API_KEY into the environment\n    from google.colab import userdata\n    os.environ[\"QORO_API_KEY\"] = userdata.get(\"QORO_API_KEY\")\nexcept ImportError:\n    pass  # not in Colab: Divi reads .env or the environment by itself\nexcept Exception as err:  # no secret yet, or notebook access switched off\n    print(f\"No QORO_API_KEY in Colab Secrets yet ({type(err).__name__}): see notebook 00, section 0.1.\")\n=== cell 3 [code] ===\n# The helper file qoblib_tools.py (instance loaders and official scorers) is downloaded from the\n# hackathon repository if it is not next to this notebook yet.\nimport urllib.request\n\nif not os.path.exists(\"qoblib_tools.py\"):\n    urllib.request.urlretrieve(\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/QoroQuantum/pushquantum-hackathon-2026/main/qoblib_tools.py\", \"qoblib_tools.py\")\nimport qoblib_tools as qt\n\nmissing = [name for name in [\n    \"load_network_demand\", \"route_min_congestion\", \"build_network_qubo\", \"repair_topology\",\n    \"route_integral\", \"write_network_solution\", \"check_network_solution\",\n] if not hasattr(qt, name)]\nif missing:\n    raise ImportError(\n        f\"{os.path.relpath(qt.__file__)} is an old copy (no {', '.join(missing)}). Delete it, restart the \"\n        \"session and run from the top: the current version is downloaded.\"\n    )\nprint(\"qoblib_tools loaded from\", os.path.relpath(qt.__file__))\n--- out: qoblib_tools loaded from qoblib_tools.py\n\n=== cell 4 [code] ===\nimport itertools\nimport time\nimport warnings\n\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\", category=SyntaxWarning)  # noisy docstrings in some dependencies\n\nimport hybrid\nimport numpy as np\nimport requests\nfrom dwave.samplers import SimulatedAnnealingSampler\nfrom IPython.display import display\n\nfrom divi.backends import JobConfig, MaestroSimulator, QoroService\nfrom divi.backends.characterization import CharacterizationOptions, characterize_and_validate\nfrom divi.qprog.algorithms import PCE, QAOA, PolyEncoding, SoftObjective\nfrom divi.qprog.ensembles import BeamSearchStrategy, PartitioningProgramEnsemble\nfrom divi.qprog.optimizers import MonteCarloOptimizer\nfrom divi.qprog.problems import (\n    BinaryOptimizationProblem,\n    CommunityDecomposer,\n    LinearConstraint,\n    QUBOPartitioningConfig,\n)\n\nprint(\"qoro-divi\", version(\"qoro-divi\"))\n--- out: qoro-divi 0.16.2\n\n=== cell 5 [code] ===\nUSE_CLOUD = False            # False: local Maestro simulator · True: Qoro cloud (uses credits)\nRUN_CHARACTERIZATION = True  # Characterization Service calls (cloud; cost scales with QUBO size)\nSHOTS = 10_000\nMC_POPULATION = 50          # Monte Carlo optimiser: circuits per iteration, sent to the backend as one batch\nMC_ITERATIONS = 10          # Monte Carlo optimiser iterations\n\ntry:\n    service = QoroService()  # reads QORO_API_KEY from .env or the environment\n    balance = service.get_credit_balance()\n    print(\"Connected to Qoro. Credit balance:\", balance.get(\"balance\", balance))\nexcept (ValueError, requests.RequestException) as err:  # no key, a wrong key, or no connection\n    service = None\n    print(f\"{err}\\nNo working API key (notebook 00, section 0.1): cloud cells will be skipped.\")\nif USE_CLOUD and service is None:\n    raise RuntimeError(\"USE_CLOUD = True needs a working API key: see notebook 00, section 0.1.\")\n\n\ndef make_backend(shots: int = SHOTS, force_sampling: bool = False):\n    # force_sampling=True: estimate everything from shots instead of exact expectation values\n    if USE_CLOUD:\n        return QoroService(\n            job_config=JobConfig(\n                shots=shots, simulator_cluster=\"qoro_maestro\", force_sampling=force_sampling\n            )\n        )\n    return MaestroSimulator(shots=shots, force_sampling=force_sampling)\n\n\nbackend = make_backend()\nprint(\"Circuits run on:\", type(backend).__name__)\n--- out: Connected to Qoro. Credit balance: 3979.89\nCircuits run on: MaestroSimulator\n\n=== cell 6 [markdown] ===\n**`solve_partitioned`** is the helper from notebook 00 (section 1.5), with one addition: an optional\n`decomposer` argument, for when you want to choose the partitions yourself.\n=== cell 7 [code] ===\ndef solve_partitioned(bqm, routine=\"qaoa\", cluster_size=12, n_layers=2, backend=backend, decomposer=None, **routine_kwargs):\n    # Partition `bqm`, solve every part with `routine`, and return the ensemble.\n    # routine: \"qaoa\" | \"iterative_qaoa\" | \"pce\" | \"progressive_pce\"\n    problem = BinaryOptimizationProblem(\n        bqm,\n        decomposer=decomposer or CommunityDecomposer(\n            QUBOPartitioningConfig(max_n_variables_per_cluster=cluster_size)\n        ),\n        composer=hybrid.SplatComposer(),\n        local_search=True,\n    )\n    ensemble = PartitioningProgramEnsemble(\n        problem=problem,\n        quantum_routine=routine,\n        n_layers=n_layers,\n        optimizer=MonteCarloOptimizer(population_size=MC_POPULATION),\n        max_iterations=MC_ITERATIONS,\n        backend=backend,\n        **routine_kwargs,\n    )\n    ensemble.run()\n    return ensemble\n=== cell 8 [markdown] ===\n---\n## 1 · QOBLIB 08: network design\n\nGiven an $n \\times n$ traffic demand matrix $t_{ij}$, build a directed network on $n$ nodes in which\n**every node has exactly 2 outgoing and 2 incoming links** (no self-loops). Route all traffic, letting\nflows split over several paths, and **minimise the largest total flow on any link**. The score is that\nmaximum × 1000, as an integer. Instances `network05` … `network24` use the top-left $n \\times n$\nblock of one fixed demand matrix.\n\nThe problem splits into two parts (credit to the parallel-tempering submission on QOBLIB):\n\n* **Choosing the topology** is combinatorial: one binary variable per possible link, $n(n-1)$ in\n  total. This is the QUBO part.\n* **Routing on a fixed topology** is a linear program that `qt.route_min_congestion` solves exactly in\n  milliseconds. It reproduces every published QOBLIB value.\n\nSo the quantum or QUBO solver *proposes* topologies, and the LP *scores* them.\n=== cell 9 [code] ===\nN_NODES = 5\ndemand = qt.load_network_demand(N_NODES)\nprint(\"Demand matrix (traffic from row node to column node):\\n\", demand)\n\nref_path = qt.fetch_qoblib_file(f\"08-network/solutions/network{N_NODES:02d}.opt.sol\")\n_, ref_arcs, _ = qt.read_network_solution(ref_path)\nprint(\"\\nPublished optimal topology:\", [(i + 1, j + 1) for i, j in ref_arcs])\nprint(\"Its score:\", qt.route_min_congestion(demand, ref_arcs).objective)\nprint(\"A random valid topology:\", qt.route_min_congestion(demand, qt.random_topology(N_NODES, np.random.default_rng(0))).objective)\n--- out: Demand matrix (traffic from row node to column node):\n [[ 0 24 43 23 21]\n [ 0  0  0  0 21]\n [16  0  0 20 44]\n [42 20 18  0 40]\n [ 0  0  0 40  0]]\n\nPublished optimal topology: [(1, 3), (1, 2), (2, 5), (2, 3), (3, 5), (3, 4), (4, 1), (4, 2), (5, 1), (5, 4)]\nIts score: 65500.0\nA random valid topology: 83000.0\n\n=== cell 10 [markdown] ===\nFor `network05` there are few enough topologies to score every one, giving a ground truth for the\npipeline below.\n=== cell 11 [code] ===\nrows = [list(itertools.combinations([j for j in range(N_NODES) if j != i], 2)) for i in range(N_NODES)]\nall_topologies = []\nfor choice in itertools.product(*rows):\n    arcs = [(i, j) for i, pair in enumerate(choice) for j in pair]\n    if qt.is_valid_topology(N_NODES, arcs):\n        all_topologies.append(arcs)\nall_scores = np.array([qt.route_min_congestion(demand, a).objective for a in all_topologies])\nprint(f\"{len(all_topologies)} valid topologies; best {all_scores.min():.0f}, median {np.median(all_scores):.0f}, worst {all_scores.max():.0f}\")\n--- out: 216 valid topologies; best 65500, median 83000, worst 115500\n\n=== cell 12 [markdown] ===\n### 1.1 A topology QUBO with Divi's `LinearConstraint`\n\n`qt.build_network_qubo` gives a **surrogate** objective. It rewards links that carry their own demand\ndirectly: $-\\sum t_{ij} x_{ij}$, scaled. The real objective needs the LP and is not a QUBO, so\n**designing a better surrogate is a big part of the challenge.**\n\nThe degree rules are linear equalities. Instead of hand-building penalties, hand them to Divi as\n`LinearConstraint`s: `BinaryOptimizationProblem` adds them as squared penalties, and\n`problem.is_feasible(bitstring)` checks them. QAOA's `get_top_solutions(feasibility=\"filter\")` keeps\nonly the feasible samples, ranked by the true objective rather than the penalised one.\n=== cell 13 [code] ===\nsurrogate, degree_penalty, arcs = qt.build_network_qubo(demand)  # arcs[v] = link of variable v\nconstraints = []\nfor node in range(N_NODES):\n    constraints.append(LinearConstraint({v: 1 for v, (i, j) in enumerate(arcs) if i == node}, \"==\", 2))  # out\n    constraints.append(LinearConstraint({v: 1 for v, (i, j) in enumerate(arcs) if j == node}, \"==\", 2))  # in\nnet_problem = BinaryOptimizationProblem(surrogate, constraints=constraints, penalty_weight=2.0)\nprint(f\"{len(arcs)} binary variables → {net_problem.cost_hamiltonian.num_qubits} qubits\")\n--- out: 20 binary variables → 20 qubits\n\n=== cell 14 [markdown] ===\n### 1.2 Characterize & validate, then run QAOA\n\nBefore spending circuits, send `net_problem` to the **Characterization Service** (notebook 00, section\n1.2) with the **published optimal topology as the reference state**. That validates the surrogate: if\nthe true optimum sits high in the QUBO's energy landscape, no sampler will find it through this QUBO,\nand the surrogate needs work. On `network05` the published optimum is the QUBO's ground state.\n\nThe constraints count as the penalty component, so the report has a `penalty` section with a\nrecommended weight, and its `structure` section lists the ten degree rules as *penalty blocks*: groups\nof 4 variables that must hold exactly 2 links each. The call can take several minutes. Divi warns that\nthe QUBO's constant offset isn't sent: the reported energies exclude it, and nothing else changes.\n=== cell 15 [code] ===\nopt_arcs = set(map(tuple, ref_arcs))  # the published optimal topology, loaded above\nref_bits = \"\".join(\"1\" if tuple(arc) in opt_arcs else \"0\" for arc in arcs)\n\nnet_report = None\nif service is not None and RUN_CHARACTERIZATION:\n    net_report = characterize_and_validate(\n        net_problem,\n        reference_states=[ref_bits],\n        service=service,\n        options=CharacterizationOptions(ansatz={\"layers\": 2}),\n    )\n    display(net_report)\n    print(\"published optimum:  \", net_report.references[0])\n    print(\"penalty section:    \", net_report.penalty)\n    print(\"penalty blocks:     \", len(net_report.structure[\"penalty_blocks\"]))\n    print(\"recommended layers: \", net_report.recommended_layers)\nelse:\n    print(\"Skipped: no API key, or RUN_CHARACTERIZATION is False.\")\n--- out: /tmp/ipykernel_50644/235482186.py:6: UserWarning: The problem carries a constant offset, which is not transmitted. The analysis is unaffected — a constant shifts every energy equally — but the reported energies exclude it.\n  net_report = characterize_and_validate(\n\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: CharacterizationResult(job_id='0e4aa6e7-bd72-4c9a-a5c1-dc3c8a22a5a3', status='COMPLETED')\n  The exact optimum is already known classically; use QAOA here to tune or benchmark it before scaling up.\n  [action] Use a penalty weight λ ≈ 0.4958: the ground state is feasible there with the widest gap to the next energy level.\n  [info] The penalty is 10 blocks each holding a group of 4 variables to a fixed count set (listed under structure): an XY mixer over each group, started from a state with that count, keeps it satisfied without its penalty terms.\n  [info] At the requested p = 2 layers QAOA's mean energy closes 68.9% of the gap from random guessing to the optimum.\n  [info] This report's QAOA results are at the submitted QUBO's λ = 2; rebuild the QUBO with λ ≈ 0.4958 and resubmit to see QAOA there.\n--- out: published optimum:   {'energy': -85.77272727272727, 'bitstring': '11000101001111001001', 'gap_to_best': 0.0, 'probability': 0.0008193272041915764}\npenalty section:     {'exact': True, 'qaoa_lambda': 2.0, 'safe_lambda': 1.3409090909090913, 'search_range': [0.01, 100.0], 'penalty_to_cost': 56.774193548387096, 'recommended_lambda': 0.49575268420302737, 'min_feasible_lambda': 0.4545454665227432}\npenalty blocks:      10\nrecommended layers:  6\n\n=== cell 16 [markdown] ===\nQAOA runs twice, as in notebook 00: **blind** (Monte Carlo from random starting angles) and, if you ran the\ncharacterization, **warm-started** from the service's angles at its recommended layer count, plus small random offsets. Every\nsample is checked against the degree rules, and every valid topology is scored by the LP.\n=== cell 17 [code] ===\ndef run_network_qaoa(label, n_layers, initial_params=None):\n    t0 = time.time()\n    qaoa_net = QAOA(\n        net_problem,\n        n_layers=n_layers,\n        optimizer=MonteCarloOptimizer(population_size=MC_POPULATION),\n        max_iterations=MC_ITERATIONS,\n        backend=make_backend(shots=20_000),\n        seed=3,\n    )\n    qaoa_net.run(initial_params=initial_params)\n    valid = qaoa_net.get_top_solutions(n=0, feasibility=\"filter\")  # n=0: every feasible sample\n    scores = {s.bitstring: qt.route_min_congestion(demand, qt.decode_topology(s.bitstring, arcs)).objective for s in valid}\n    p_opt = sum(s.prob for s in valid if scores[s.bitstring] == all_scores.min())\n    best = min(scores.values())\n    print(f\"{label:<24} {qaoa_net.total_circuit_count:>3} circuits, {time.time() - t0:>3.0f}s | {len(valid)} valid topologies, \"\n          f\"{sum(s.prob for s in valid):5.1%} of the probability mass | P(optimal topology) {p_opt:.3%} | \"\n          f\"best sampled: {best:.0f}, rank {(all_scores < best).sum() + 1} of {len(all_topologies)}\")\n\n\nrun_network_qaoa(\"QAOA p=2, blind\", n_layers=2)\ninit = net_report.qaoa_initial_params() if net_report is not None else None\nif init is not None:\n    p = init.shape[1] // 2\n    # One starting point per population member: the service's angles plus small random offsets.\n    starts = init + np.random.default_rng(3).normal(scale=0.05, size=(MC_POPULATION, 2 * p))\n    starts[0] = init[0]\n    run_network_qaoa(f\"QAOA p={p}, warm-started\", n_layers=p, initial_params=starts)\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: QAOA p=2, blind          501 circuits,  58s | 180 valid topologies,  2.0% of the probability mass | P(optimal topology) 0.005% | best sampled: 65500, rank 1 of 216\n\n--- out: QAOA p=6, warm-started   501 circuits, 128s | 216 valid topologies, 75.5% of the probability mass | P(optimal topology) 0.580% | best sampled: 65500, rank 1 of 216\n\n=== cell 18 [markdown] ===\n### 1.3 Scale up: `network12`\n\n132 binary variables. We compare four ways of proposing topologies, each scored by the LP:\n\n* random valid topologies,\n* simulated annealing on the surrogate QUBO,\n* partitioned QAOA on the surrogate QUBO,\n* PCE on the *whole* surrogate QUBO: 132 variables on 16 qubits.\n\nSamples can break the degree rules. Partitions cut straight through them, and single-bit flips\ncan't fix a node with one link too many when the other end already has exactly two.\n`qt.repair_topology` maps any sample to the closest valid topology, keeping as many of its links as\npossible, so every sample becomes a scorable candidate.\n=== cell 19 [code] ===\nN_BIG, BKV_NET = 12, 276474\ndemand_big = qt.load_network_demand(N_BIG)\nsur_big, pen_big, arcs_big = qt.build_network_qubo(demand_big)\nnet_bqm = sur_big.copy()\npen = pen_big.copy()\npen.scale(1.0)  # penalty weight\nnet_bqm.update(pen)\n\n\ndef best_topology(samples, arcs, demand):\n    # Decode → repair → LP-score every sample; return (best score, best arcs, #distinct topologies).\n    n = len(demand)\n    topologies = {\n        tuple(sorted(qt.repair_topology(n, qt.decode_topology(s, arcs), demand))) for s in samples\n    }\n    scores = {t: qt.route_min_congestion(demand, t).objective for t in topologies}\n    best = min(scores, key=scores.get)\n    return scores[best], list(best), len(topologies)\n\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nrandom_best = min(qt.route_min_congestion(demand_big, qt.random_topology(N_BIG, rng)).objective for _ in range(300))\nprint(f\"Random (300 topologies):          {random_best:.0f}\")\n\nsa_net = SimulatedAnnealingSampler().sample(net_bqm, num_reads=300, seed=1)\nsa_net_score, sa_net_arcs, n_valid = best_topology(sa_net.samples(), arcs_big, demand_big)\nprint(f\"Simulated annealing on surrogate: {sa_net_score:.0f} ({n_valid} distinct topologies)\")\n\nt0 = time.time()\nnet_bqm_n = net_bqm.copy()\nnet_bqm_n.normalize()\nnet_ensemble = solve_partitioned(net_bqm_n, \"qaoa\", cluster_size=12)\nnet_tops = net_ensemble.get_top_solutions(\n    n=100, strategy=BeamSearchStrategy(beam_width=100, n_partition_candidates=10)\n)\nq_net_score, q_net_arcs, n_valid = best_topology(\n    [dict(zip(net_bqm_n.variables, solution)) for solution, _ in net_tops], arcs_big, demand_big\n)\nprint(f\"Partitioned QAOA on surrogate:    {q_net_score:.0f} ({n_valid} distinct topologies, {time.time() - t0:.0f}s)\")\n\nt0 = time.time()\npce_net = PCE(\n    BinaryOptimizationProblem(net_bqm_n),\n    encoding=PolyEncoding(),\n    objective=SoftObjective(alpha=2.0),\n    n_layers=2,\n    optimizer=MonteCarloOptimizer(population_size=MC_POPULATION),\n    max_iterations=MC_ITERATIONS,\n    backend=backend,\n    seed=1,\n)\npce_net.run()\npce_tops = pce_net.get_top_solutions(n=100, min_prob=0.0, sort_by=\"energy\")\npce_net_score, pce_net_arcs, n_valid = best_topology([s.bitstring for s in pce_tops], arcs_big, demand_big)\nprint(f\"PCE on surrogate ({pce_net.n_qubits} qubits):     {pce_net_score:.0f} ({n_valid} distinct topologies, {time.time() - t0:.0f}s)\")\nprint(f\"Best known:                       {BKV_NET}\")\n--- out: Random (300 topologies):          328400\nSimulated annealing on surrogate: 309750 (300 distinct topologies)\n\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: Output()\n--- out: Partitioned QAOA on surrogate:    347000 (68 distinct topologies, 40s)\n\n--- out: PCE on surrogate (16 qubits):     313778 (100 distinct topologies, 8s)\nBest known:                       276474\n\n=== cell 20 [markdown] ===\n### 1.4 Write a submission file\n\nThe checker wants **integer** flows, in units of 1/1000. `qt.route_integral` finds integer flows that\nachieve the LP score, and `qt.check_network_solution` is a port of QOBLIB's checker.\n=== cell 21 [code] ===\nbest_net_score, best_net_arcs = min(\n    (sa_net_score, sa_net_arcs), (q_net_score, q_net_arcs), (pce_net_score, pce_net_arcs), key=lambda x: x[0]\n)\nrouting = qt.route_integral(demand_big, best_net_arcs)\nnet_path = f\"network{N_BIG:02d}.sol\"\nz = qt.write_network_solution(net_path, N_BIG, routing)\nprint(f\"Wrote {net_path} with objective {z}\")\nprint(\"Checker:\", qt.check_network_solution(net_path, N_BIG, demand_big))\n--- out: Wrote network12.sol with objective 309750\nChecker: (True, 309750, 'valid')\n\n=== cell 22 [markdown] ===\n### 1.5 Targets and ideas\n\nBest-known values from the [QOBLIB table](https://github.com/ZIB-AOPT/QOBLIB/blob/main/08-network/solutions/README.md) (check it for updates).\n\n| Instance | Variables | Best known | Status |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| `network05` … `network10` | 20 – 90 | 65 500 … 210 800 | proven optimal: match them |\n| `network11`, `network13` … `network23` | 110 – 506 | 238 334 … 659 369 | best known, set by a parallel-tempering submission |\n| `network12` | 132 | 276 474 | best known (reference) |\n| `network24` | 552 | 663 688 | best known (reference) |\n\n**Ideas to try**\n* **Better surrogates.** Reward two-hop paths $x_{ij}x_{jk}$ for large $t_{ik}$. Penalise nodes that\n  send a lot of traffic over few links. Learn the weights from LP results.\n* **LP feedback (Benders-style).** The LP's dual values tell you which links are congested. Re-weight\n  the QUBO and re-solve in a loop.\n* **Feasible-by-construction encodings.** A valid topology is two permutations with no fixed points\n  that never agree. One-hot permutation encodings with an XY mixer keep QAOA inside the feasible space. The report in section 1.2 suggests the same: an XY mixer over each\n  penalty block, started from a state with 2 links.\n* **Beat SA first.** On `network12` the plain quantum pipelines above are far from the best known\n  value, and simulated annealing on the same surrogate is a strong bar. Closing that gap with better surrogates, penalties, depth or encodings is a good first goal.\n* **Quantum proposals inside a local search.** Use QAOA or PCE samples to propose degree-preserving\n  2-exchanges, $(a\\to b), (c\\to d) \\Rightarrow (a\\to d), (c\\to b)$, scored by the LP.\n* **Characterize at scale.** Section 1.2 characterizes `network05`. Do the same for the instance you\n  are pushing: read `structure` to choose partitions and `penalty` for the weight, and pass your best\n  topology as a reference state to check that your surrogate ranks it well.\n=== cell 23 [markdown] ===\n---\n## Handing in\n\nKeep every `.sol` file you write, with the instance, its size and the method and settings that produced\nit. **Handing in** at the end of notebook 00 says how to submit.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"2c81bffe-270f-4567-83dc-9bdfc3a2dbe6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/danie/quantum-icing-qoro","root":"/home/danie/quantum-icing-qoro","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"LorenzoLaCorte/quantum-icing-qoro","visibility":"unknown"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"LorenzoLaCorte/quantum-icing-qoro","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"LorenzoLaCorte/quantum-icing-qoro","visibility":"unknown"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/danie/quantum-icing-qoro","platform":"wsl"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"169c3c60-64e4-4f18-9b39-be33999ce4ee","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"exte