y measured, trust it)\n- Repo: /home/danie/quantum-icing-qoro. `source .venv/bin/activate`; run Python with `PYTHONPATH=.:solutions/agent_qcompound`. Your working folder: /home/danie/quantum-icing-qoro/solutions/agent_qcompound/small/ (create it). Do NOT read or use other `solutions/agent_*` folders (user's instruction). You may import `qoblib_tools` (qt), `network_ils.WarmLP`, `network_ils.move_2ex` from the repo root, and `shrink`, `bank`, `memguard` from solutions/agent_qcompound.\n- Problem networkNN: NN nodes, directed graph, each node out-degree 2 and in-degree 2, no self-loops (2·NN arcs). Demand = top-left NN×NN block (`qt.load_network_demand(NN)`). Score = LP min of the max arc load (splittable multicommodity routing) ×1000, ceil; lower is better. BKVs: 23: 659369, 22: 618135, 21: 569273, 20: 528528, 19: 492924, 18: 473375 (dict `bank.BKV`).\n- KEY TRICK (already works): deleting the last node from a good (NN+1)-node topology and splicing its 2 in-neighbours to its 2 out-neighbours gives a strong NN-node start: `z, arcs = shrink.shrink(arcs_big, NN+1, qt.load_network_demand(NN))`. From our network24 best (solutions_best/network24.sol, 657121) this already gave network23 = 642084, −2.62% vs BKV, banked at solutions/agent_qcompound/network23.sol. Chaining without search degrades: 22: 623250 (+0.83%), 21: 589250 (+3.5%), 20: 548759 (+3.8%). So: polish each instance with search, then derive the next smaller one from the POLISHED topology (chain 23 → 22 → 21 → 20 …).\n- Fast exact scorer: `W = WarmLP(D, arcs)`; `z, du = W.solve(arcs)` (LP value + arc dual prices); `basis = W.h.getBasis()`; `W.solve(new, basis=basis)` ≈ 15–22 ms per neighbour; `W.solve(new, cutoff=X, basis=basis)` returns (None, None) early if LP ≥ X (use cutoff=current z for fast descent). `move_2ex(arcs, p, q)`: slots p,q (a,b),(c,d) → (a,d),(c,b), None if self-loop; also reject if `len(set(new)) < len(new)`. Working example: solutions/agent_qcompound/probe1.py. WarmLP is built for a fixed number of slots (one instance size), so create a new WarmLP per instance.\n- Divi QAOA works locally: solutions/agent_qcompound/qaoa_probe.py (20 qubits, p=2, MonteCarloOptimizer(population_size=20), max_iterations=5, MaestroSimulator(shots=4000) → 101 circuits, 10.5 s, ~500 MB RSS). Verify bitstring order ↔ BQM variable mapping before trusting decoded samples.\n\n## RAM (critical — 7.4 GB machine, it froze before)\nAt most 2 heavy Python processes for you at the same time. Every long script starts with `import memguard; memguard.start(min_mb=900)`. Check `free -m` before launching. No multiprocessing pools.\n\n## Method\nPer instance: (1) start from the derived topology; (2) 2-exchange descent (first- or best-improvement with LP cutoff) to a local optimum; (3) escape with a Quantum Compound Move step: take N=16–20 lowest-Δ 2-exchanges (mostly mutually compatible), LP the compatible pairs to get exact interactions J_ij = z_ij − z0 − Δ_i − Δ_j, build the QUBO E(y)=ΣΔ_i y_i + ΣJ_ij y_i y_j + penalty on conflicting pairs, sample it with Divi QAOA (p=2, small MC budget), LP-evaluate the top ~30 conflict-free multi-move samples; accept improvements, otherwise do an ILS kick (accept best compound within +1.5%) and descend again; restart from best periodically. (4) Bank every new best with `bank.bank(NN, arcs, '<method>', 'solutions/agent_qcompound/small')` (integral routing + official checker + log.csv) — bank as soon as an instance goes below its BKV, then keep improving. Record for each improvement whether it came from descent (classical) or from the QAOA compound step (honesty matters).\nSuggested split: process 1 runs the chain 23 → 22 → 21 → 20 (≈5 min per instance: bank quickly, then move on; network23 is already below BKV, so give it only a short polish before deriving 22); process 2 starts at network22 derived from the banked network23 (solutions/agent_qcompound/network23.sol) with a different seed and continues down 21 → 20 → 19, or targets 20/19 directly. Avoid both